From: panvk-kbase-android worker <panvk-worker@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH csf-v11 174] panvk: decode BC2/BC3 into AFRC-compressed RGBA8 shadows
Generated-by: LLM

BC2 and BC3 go through the ASTC 4x4 transcode at 8 bpp, which costs an
encoder search per block and loses quality on real alpha textures
(34-44 dB on Assassin's Creed Rogue BC3 against 45-56 dB for a 16 bpp
shadow). Where the GPU has AFRC, decode them instead into an internal
RGBA8 colour image with fixed-rate AFRC: 24-byte coding units (12 bpp)
for BC2, whose colour is a 565 palette and alpha 4-bit, and 32-byte
units (16 bpp) for BC3.

AFRC is only produced by the tile writeback, so the decode is a layered
fragment pass over each copy region (new BC2/BC3 fragment decoder; BC3
alpha uses the integer D3D palette). Uploads decode straight from the
copy source, image copies and ZERO_INITIALIZED transitions from the raw
plane. The shadow shares the decoded plane's memory after the raw
blocks. Based on the zenithblue-oss csf-v11/150, 151 and 180 design,
limited to BC2/BC3: BC1 stays on ASTC (mostly opaque, where ASTC is
smaller, faster and closer), BC4/BC5 on exact R8/RG8, BC6H/BC7 on
ASTC. Cube-compatible, sparse and non-2D images keep the transcode.

PANVK_BC_AFRC=16/24/32 picks the coding-unit size, 0 keeps BC2/BC3 on
the ASTC transcode (lowest memory).

Also take the AFRC row-stride fix from the same series:
pan_mod_afrc_init_slice_layout() sized rows from the mip width rounded
down to whole AFRC tiles, so levels narrower than a tile overlapped.
biblioklept

Tested GPU: Mali-G615 MC2 (OPPO CPH2763, MT6878)
Tested Kbase UAPI: 1.21
Dependencies: csf-v11/173

